-- title:
--   Existence of the canonical morphism to Proj of a section ring
-- statement:
--   Fix a commutative ring $S$, a scheme $X$, a morphism $f : X \to \operatorname{Spec} S$, a module $L$ over $X$, and a commutative $S$-algebra $R$ graded by submodules $\mathfrak{R} : \mathbb{N} \to \mathrm{Submodule}\ S\ R$, together with maps $\iota_n : \mathfrak{R}_n \to \Gamma(L^{\otimes n}, \top)$ into the global sections of the tensor powers $L^{\otimes 0} = \mathbf{1}$, $L^{\otimes (n+1)} = L^{\otimes n} \otimes L$. Three hypotheses are imposed. First, $(R,\mathfrak R,\iota)$ is a section ring for $f$ and $L$: each $\iota_n$ is bijective and additive, $\iota_n(s\cdot x) = \mathrm{baseScalar}(f,s)\cdot \iota_n(x)$ for $s \in S$, $\iota_0(1)$ is the unit section $1$, and $\iota_{m+n}(xy)$ is the image of the tensor product section $\iota_m(x)\otimes\iota_n(y)$ under the canonical isomorphism $L^{\otimes m}\otimes L^{\otimes n}\cong L^{\otimes(m+n)}$. Second, $L$ is invertible: every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ on which the restriction of $L$ is isomorphic to the unit sheaf of modules. Third, $L$ admits a closed immersion by sections over $f$: for some $N$ there are global sections $\sigma_0,\dots,\sigma_N$ of $L$ and a morphism $X \to \mathbb{P}^N_S$ over $f$ which is a closed immersion, each $\sigma_i$ being a frame over the preimage of $D_+(X_i)$ and the ratios $X_j/X_i$ pulling back to the transition relations between the $\sigma_i$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a morphism $\theta : X \to \operatorname{Proj} \mathfrak{R}$ which is canonical in the following sense: $\theta$ followed by $\operatorname{Proj}\mathfrak R \to \operatorname{Spec} \mathfrak R_0$ and by the map induced by $S \to R \to \mathfrak R_0$ equals $f$; for every $n > 0$ and every $\sigma \in \mathfrak{R}_n$ the section $\iota_n(\sigma)$ is a frame on $\theta^{-1}D_+(\sigma)$, i.e. multiplication by it is bijective from functions to sections of $L^{\otimes n}$ on every open subset of that preimage; and for all $n > 0$, $\sigma \in \mathfrak{R}_n$, $k \in \mathbb{N}$ and $s \in \mathfrak{R}_{kn}$, the pullback along $\theta$ of the degree-zero function $s/\sigma^k$ on $D_+(\sigma)$ multiplies $\iota_{kn}(\sigma^k)$ into $\iota_{kn}(s)$ after restriction to $\theta^{-1}D_+(\sigma)$.
--
--   This is the existence half of the classical construction of the morphism to the $\operatorname{Proj}$ of a graded ring of sections of a line bundle, in the form of EGA II 3.7.1 and Hartshorne II.7.1(b), here with the bundle assumed invertible and generated by degree-one sections presenting a closed immersion into a projective space over the base. It supplies the comparison morphism used in the descent statement [`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_descent_of_faithfullyFlat_of_cocycle`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.exists_descent_of_faithfullyFlat_of_cocycle).
